\title{Rotated Laplacians, Periodicity Ratios, and a Quantization Theorem
for Nearly-Periodic Digraphs}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the structure theory of the \emph{periodicity ratio} of a finite
weighted digraph, a quantity defined through the spectrum of the family of
\emph{rotated Laplacian matrices} $D - A_\omega$, where $A_\omega$ is the
arc-weight matrix scaled by a complex rotation $\omega$ of modulus one. The
periodicity ratio at an integer $p \ge 1$ is the infimum, over nonzero vectors
whose coordinates are $0$ or powers of the primitive $p$-th root of unity
$\omega_p = e^{2\pi i/p}$, of the Rayleigh quotient
$\bigl(\sum_{u,v} w_{uv}\|x_v - \omega_p x_u\|^2\bigr)/\bigl(\sum_v d_v
\|x_v\|^2\bigr)$; for $p = 2$ it specializes to the bipartiteness ratio.
We prove: (i) for a strongly connected nonnegatively weighted digraph, a
unimodular $p$-phase vector of zero rotated energy exists if and only if $p$
divides the length of every closed walk, so that the zero set of the
periodicity ratio is exactly the divisor lattice of the period; we also exhibit
a strongly connected digraph with vanishing $2$-ratio and no closed walk of
length $2$, refuting the natural strengthening. (ii) The rotated Rayleigh
quotient never exceeds $2$, and the periodicity ratio is invariant under
reversal of all arcs. (iii) For the chain weighting $w_{uv} = \pi_u P_{uv}$
induced by a stationary distribution, right eigenvectors of $P$ with unimodular
eigenvalue are exactly the zero-energy vectors (the converse requiring
$\pi > 0$). (iv) \emph{Quantization}: if every nonzero arc weight is at least
$1$ and $p \ge 2$, the rotated energy of a unimodular $p$-phase vector is either
exactly $0$ or at least $4\sin^2(\pi/p)$; hence energy strictly below that
threshold forces exact $p$-periodicity. The weight hypothesis is necessary:
rescaled directed $4$-cycles have arbitrarily small positive $3$-energy.
\end{abstract}

